  "hero.title": "How Containers Isolate a Process",
  "hero.kicker": "Computing · Software principles · A hands-on long essay",
  "hero.sub": "One `docker run` command conjures up a \"clean Linux machine\".<br>Yet there is no \"container\" object inside the Linux kernel—what did it actually lock in a sandbox?",
  "hero.lead": "Type `docker run --rm -m 512m --cpus=0.5 python:3.12-slim python app.py`, and milliseconds later a process runs with what looks like its own root directory, network card, and `PID 1`. Search the entire Linux kernel source tree, however, and you will never find a `struct container`. This essay asks one question: <strong>what does that command actually ask the kernel to assemble, why can ten copies of a 200 MiB image share 199 MiB on disk, and what can still leak across the shared host kernel?</strong> We follow the real lifecycle from launch to shutdown: first how the runtime toolchain unpacks layers and calls into the kernel, then how SHA-256 content addressing deduplicates images and OverlayFS builds a writable filesystem illusion, then how eight namespaces filter a process's view, how virtual cables bridge packets out, how `cgroups v2` throttles CPUs or kills on memory, and finally what `seccomp-bpf` and PID 1 actually guard.",
  "hero.note": "The structure follows <strong>OCI Image Spec v1.1.0</strong> (released 15 February 2024), <strong>OCI Runtime Spec v1.2.0</strong> (released 13 February 2024), and <strong>Linux kernel 6.x primitives</strong> (`namespaces(7)`, `cgroups(7)` v2, `overlayfs`, `seccomp(2)`, `capabilities(7)`). This page does not run a live Docker daemon (authored on a macOS Darwin host with neither a Linux kernel nor `dockerd`): nine interactive models reimplement the exact algorithms in `models.js`, while `tools/crosscheck.cjs` checks our pure-JS FIPS 180-4 SHA-256 and OCI Manifest / Config / ChainID digests byte-for-byte against Node `node:crypto` alongside all kernel arithmetic invariants. Background concepts live in <a href=\"../../systems/how-the-os-talks-to-hardware/\">How the OS Talks to Hardware</a> and <a href=\"../../systems/how-virtual-memory-works/\">How Virtual Memory Works</a>. Each chapter ends by stating what its model leaves out.",
  "ch1.title": "There Is No \"Container\" in the Kernel",
  "toc.ch1": "No Container in the Kernel",
  "ch1.law": "A container is not a miniature virtual machine. To the host Linux kernel, it is just an ordinary process wearing three harnesses: Namespaces limit what it can see, cgroups v2 limit how much hardware it can burn, and OverlayFS plus seccomp limit which files and syscalls it can touch.",
  "ch1.p1": "Here is that one-line command and the four steps that actually cross the system-call boundary into the Linux kernel. When you type `docker run`, the `docker` CLI merely hands flags to `containerd`, which spawns a lightweight `containerd-shim` process (so restarting the Docker daemon does not kill running workloads). An OCI runtime such as `runc` then reads `config.json`, calls `clone3`, `pivot_root`, writes `cgroup.procs`, locks down `prctl(PR_SET_SECCOMP)`, and finally `execve`s into `python app.py`.",
  "ch1.code.aria": "One docker run command and the OCI runtime syscalls beneath it",
  "ch1.code": "docker run --rm -m 512m --cpus=0.5 python:3.12-slim python app.py\n\n# containerd-shim -> runc reads OCI config.json and asks the kernel to:\n# 1. clone3(CLONE_NEWPID | CLONE_NEWNS | CLONE_NEWNET | CLONE_NEWUTS | CLONE_NEWIPC | CLONE_NEWCGROUP)\n# 2. mount(\"overlay\", merged, \"overlay\", \"lowerdir=...,upperdir=...,workdir=...\") + pivot_root()\n# 3. write(\"/sys/fs/cgroup/docker/<id>/cpu.max\", \"50000 100000\") + memory.max = 536870912\n# 4. prctl(PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS, 1) + seccomp(SECCOMP_SET_MODE_FILTER, &prog) + execve(\"python\")",
  "ch1.p2": "Each layer down strips away higher-level nouns. The CLI speaks image names and `--cpus=0.5`; `containerd` tracks content-addressed tar blobs and manages shims; `runc` turns `config.json` into Linux syscalls and pseudo-file writes; inside the kernel itself, \"images\" and \"containers\" do not exist—only <strong>threads, namespace pointers, cgroup accounting files, VFS mount trees, and BPF filter programs</strong>.",
  "dg.stack.cap": "Figure 1. Four layers crossed by one docker run command. Below the syscall boundary sits one shared Linux kernel serving every container on the host (see <a href=\"../../systems/how-the-os-talks-to-hardware/#ch1\">chapter 1 of How the OS Talks to Hardware</a> on privilege rings).",
  "dg.stack.aria": "Diagram: four layers, docker CLI, containerd and runc, syscall boundary, and the shared Linux kernel",
  "dg.stack.l1": "docker CLI",
  "dg.stack.l1s": "Parses flags & environment",
  "dg.stack.l2": "containerd + shim + runc",
