  "dg.stack.l3": "Syscall boundary",
  "dg.stack.l3s": "clone3, mount/pivot_root, seccomp",
  "dg.stack.l4": "Shared Linux kernel",
  "dg.stack.l4s": "Namespaces · cgroups v2 · OverlayFS",
  "dg.stack.x1": "gRPC / CRI call",
  "dg.stack.x2": "OCI config.json + rootfs",
  "dg.stack.x3": "Hooks onto task_struct",
  "ch1.p3": "This marks the bright line between containers and hardware virtual machines. A hypervisor VM (such as QEMU/KVM) relies on CPU hardware virtualization (<a href=\"../../systems/how-virtual-memory-works/#ch11\">chapter 11 of How Virtual Memory Works</a>) to boot a separate guest kernel, guest page tables, and virtual devices per instance. Isolation is hardware-backed, at the cost of seconds of boot time and a full kernel's memory footprint per guest. Containers discard that second kernel altogether, starting in milliseconds while sharing the single host kernel underneath.",
  "ch1.model.title": "Isolation Boundaries: Memory Footprint & Boot Ledger",
