  "ch1.say.process": "{n} bare processes share one kernel at <em>{mem} MiB</em> total and start in ~{boot} ms, leaving all {sub}/10 host subsystems fully exposed.",
  "ch1.say.container": "{n} OCI containers still share the single host kernel (only {over} MiB shim overhead each), totaling <em>{mem} MiB</em> and booting in ~{boot} ms, while {sub}/10 host kernel surfaces remain shared.",
  "ch1.say.microvm": "{n} MicroVMs (modeled on Firecracker numbers) give each workload its own slim guest kernel and minimal VMM, taking <em>{mem} MiB</em> and booting in ~{boot} ms with only {sub}/10 hypercall surface shared.",
  "ch1.say.vm": "{n} full VMs pack a complete guest kernel and full emulation into every instance (<em>{mem} MiB</em> total, ~{boot} ms boot), cutting kernel sharing down at steep memory cost.",
  "ch1.p4": "Check the numbers at 8 instances: 8 OCI containers take 329.6 MiB and launch in roughly 15 ms each; 8 full VMs consume 1,584 MiB at roughly 1,800 ms boot each (while a MicroVM trims boot down to ~115 ms). Dropping per-instance kernels buys density and speed; the rest of this essay explains how files, PIDs, packets, CPU slices, memory, and syscalls are locked down inside that single shared kernel.",
  "ch1.limit": "The model leaves out: cross-container page cache savings (covered in chapters 2 and 7), demand-paged kernel modules, and memory ballooning; boot timings and base footprints are order-of-magnitude model parameters rather than a single machine's benchmark.",
  "ch2.title": "OCI Image Layers and Content-Addressable Digests",
  "toc.ch2": "OCI Image Layers & ChainID",
  "ch2.law": "A container image is not a monolithic disk snapshot; it is an ordered list of read-only tar archives linked by SHA-256 digests. Whenever two services share the same lower layers, the host stores those bytes exactly once.",
  "ch2.p1": "When `containerd` pulls `python:3.12-slim`, it fetches a tree of <strong>SHA-256 content-addressed</strong> JSON objects (OCI Image Spec v1.1.0). First comes an <strong>Image Index</strong> matching CPU architectures (such as `linux/amd64` or `linux/arm64`) to an <strong>Image Manifest</strong>. The Manifest points to an <strong>Image Config</strong> (command, environment variables, and unpacked layer `diff_ids`) plus a stack of read-only compressed tar layers.",
  "dg.oci.cap": "Figure 2. Pointer chain in OCI Image Spec v1.1.0 and the ChainID recurrence. Changing one byte in layer L3 leaves ChainIDs for L0 through L2 completely untouched.",
  "dg.oci.aria": "Diagram: OCI Image Index pointing to Manifest, Config, and layers L0 to L3 linked by ChainID",
  "dg.oci.idx": "Image Index (multi-arch)",
  "dg.oci.idxs": "linux/amd64 · linux/arm64",
  "dg.oci.mref": "sha256 -> Manifest",
  "dg.oci.man": "Image Manifest",
  "dg.oci.mans": "config + layers[] digests",
  "dg.oci.cref": "sha256 -> Config",
  "dg.oci.cfg": "Image Config",
  "dg.oci.cfgs": "rootfs.diff_ids[L0..L3]",
  "dg.oci.chain": "ChainID = sha256(prev + DiffID)",
  "dg.oci.l0": "debian:12-slim (124 MiB)",
  "dg.oci.l1": "python:3.12 (48 MiB)",
  "dg.oci.l2": "pip-deps (27 MiB)",
  "dg.oci.l3": "app.py (1 MiB)",
  "ch2.p2": "Checking a single layer's unpacked tar digest (`DiffID`) alone is not enough, because identical pip packages unpacked onto Debian vs Alpine yield different root filesystems. The OCI spec therefore defines <strong>ChainID</strong>: `ChainID(L0) = DiffID(L0)`, and for every higher layer `ChainID(Lk) = sha256(ChainID(Lk-1) + \" \" + DiffID(Lk))`. That single formula creates an asymmetry: <strong>changing a lower layer invalidates every ChainID above it</strong>, whereas editing only the top application layer leaves every base ChainID identical.",
  "ch2.model.title": "Real SHA-256 OCI Layer & ChainID Deduplication",
  "ch2.model.how": "Choose which layer mutates and how many distinct services share the host, then inspect real 64-hex SHA-256 ChainIDs and watch how cache hits propagate into on-disk savings.",
