  "ch2.read.shared": "Shared base bytes",
  "ch2.read.stored": "Actual disk stored",
  "ch2.read.naive": "Naive copy total",
  "ch2.read.hits": "Layer hits",
  "ch2.say.none": "When {n} Python services differ only in L3 (1 MiB), L0–L2 (<em>{shared} MiB</em>) share identical ChainIDs—occupying just {stored} MiB on disk instead of {naive} MiB (saving {saved} MiB).",
  "ch2.say.l3": "Editing 1 byte in L3 changes L3's digest, while the <em>{shared} MiB ChainIDs</em> below stay intact; {n} variants add only 1 MiB each at the top.",
  "ch2.say.l2": "Changing L2 invalidates L2's ChainID and cascades into <em>rebuilding L3 too</em>, shrinking shared storage to {shared} MiB ({stored} MiB total).",
  "ch2.say.l1": "Upgrading L1 near the bottom breaks the ChainID chain from L1 upward, leaving only L0 (124 MiB) shared across {n} variants and swelling disk usage to <em>{stored} MiB</em>.",
  "ch2.p3": "Now we can answer the disk-sharing puzzle from the hero: with a `124 + 48 + 27 + 1 =` 200 MiB image, two services differing only in their top 1 MiB layer take 201 MiB instead of 400 MiB (199 MiB shared). Across ten variants on one host, naive copies burn 2,000 MiB while content addressing stores 209 MiB—sharing 199 MiB identically on disk (and ten containers of the exact same image share all 200 MiB of read-only layers with zero extra copies). That SHA-256 chain is why Dockerfile guides tell you to put volatile `COPY .` lines at the very bottom.",
  "ch2.limit": "The model leaves out: compression ratios between compressed tar `Digest` and unpacked `DiffID`, empty metadata-only layers, and the thin per-container writable layer examined in chapter 3.",
  "ch3.title": "OverlayFS and `pivot_root`: Layered Illusion on Disk",
  "toc.ch3": "OverlayFS & Copy-Up",
  "ch3.law": "A container's `/` filesystem is not copied on startup. The Linux kernel's OverlayFS merges a stack of read-only directories (lowerdir) with one private writable directory (upperdir): reads fall through, writes copy the whole file up first, and deletes write a zero-byte whiteout marker.",
  "ch3.p1": "Unpacked OCI layers sit on disk strictly read-only so ten containers never corrupt a shared `/usr/bin/python`. OverlayFS stacks four paths: read-only `lowerdir=L1:L0`, one private `upperdir` per container, an empty `workdir` on the same filesystem for atomic renames, and the combined `merged` view, into which `pivot_root` moves the process's `/` root (unlike memory page-level copy-on-write in <a href=\"../../systems/how-virtual-memory-works/#ch9\">chapter 9 of How Virtual Memory Works</a>, OverlayFS operates at <strong>whole-file</strong> granularity).",
  "dg.overlay.cap": "Figure 3. OverlayFS directory stacking: reads fall down to lowerdir, modifications trigger whole-file Copy-Up into upperdir, and deletions place whiteout markers instead of touching read-only layers.",
  "dg.overlay.aria": "Diagram: lowerdir read-only stack, upperdir writable layer, and workdir merging into the container's merged root view",
  "dg.overlay.low": "lowerdir (L0+L1 read-only)",
  "dg.overlay.lows": "python · model.bin 100M · pkg.tar",
  "dg.overlay.up": "upperdir (writable)",
  "dg.overlay.ups": "copy-up model.bin + .wh.pkg.tar",
  "dg.overlay.wk": "workdir (atomic rename staging)",
  "dg.overlay.mount": "mount -t overlay -> pivot_root",
  "dg.overlay.mg": "merged (container / view)",
  "dg.overlay.mg1": "model.bin -> served from upperdir",
  "dg.overlay.mg2": "pkg.tar -> hidden by whiteout",
  "ch3.p2": "Two subtle behaviors explain most container storage surprises. First is <strong>Copy-Up</strong>: opening a 100 MiB lower-layer file to append even 4 KiB—or simply running `chmod +x` on its permission bits—forces the kernel to <strong>copy all 100 MiB into `upperdir` first</strong> before modifying the copy. Second is <strong>Whiteout</strong>: because lower layers cannot be altered, deleting a lower file in a subsequent `RUN` or runtime session creates a 0-byte `0/0` char device (or `.wh.&lt;name&gt;` in OCI tar archives; recreating a directory writes `.wh..wh..opq`). The file disappears from `ls`, yet all 80 MiB stay locked in the lower layer forever.",
  "ch3.model.title": "OverlayFS Ledger: Copy-Up & Whiteout Cost",
  "ch3.model.how": "Select six real file operations or Dockerfile patterns and inspect lowerdir, upperdir, and merged views to see when 100 MiB copies silently and when deleting a file saves zero bytes.",
