  "ch3.op.rm-same": "Same RUN && rm 80M tar",
  "ch3.op.opaque": "Recreate /var/cache dir",
  "ch3.cv.aria": "Three horizontal panels showing files stored in lowerdir, upperdir, and the resulting merged view",
  "ch3.tier.lower": "lowerdir (L0 + L1 read-only layers)",
  "ch3.tier.upper": "upperdir (writable / new layer)",
  "ch3.tier.merged": "merged (visible inside container /)",
  "ch3.empty": "(empty — zero writes yet)",
  "ch3.read.copy": "Copy-Up traffic",
  "ch3.read.upper": "upperdir size",
  "ch3.read.total": "Total stored bytes",
  "ch3.read.waste": "Hidden dead bytes",
  "ch3.say.read": "Reading lower-layer files serves straight from shared L0/L1 page cache: upperdir uses <em>{upper} MiB</em> and Copy-Up is {copy} MiB.",
  "ch3.say.append": "Appending just 4 KiB to a 100 MiB lower file triggers a full <em>{copy} MiB Copy-Up</em>; both copies sit on disk, pushing total storage to {total} MiB.",
  "ch3.say.chmod": "Even a single-bit <code>chmod +x</code> forces the kernel to copy all <em>{copy} MiB</em> up into upperdir so inode metadata can be written.",
  "ch3.say.rm-split": "Running <code>rm pkg.tar</code> in a separate <code>RUN</code> layer writes a 0-byte whiteout <code>.wh.pkg.tar</code>: the container sees {vis} MiB, while <em>{waste} MiB</em> stay trapped in L1 (total {total} MiB).",
  "ch3.say.rm-same": "Chaining <code>curl ... &amp;&amp; rm pkg.tar</code> in the same <code>RUN</code> unlinks the file before snapshot commit—zero whiteout needed, cutting total stored size to <em>{total} MiB</em>.",
  "ch3.say.opaque": "Recreating the directory in upperdir places an opaque marker <code>.wh..wh..opq</code> hiding all lower entries (<em>{waste} MiB</em> hidden, {total} MiB still stored).",
  "ch3.p3": "Compare those two delete paths: deleting `pkg.tar` in a second `RUN` step leaves an image of 185.2 MiB, whereas chaining `&& rm` in the same `RUN` shrinks it to 105.2 MiB (saving 80 MiB). And for databases or large model weights written at runtime, mounting a host <strong>Volume / Bind Mount</strong> bypasses OverlayFS completely so writes never stall on Copy-Up.",
  "ch3.limit": "The model leaves out: `redirect_dir` metadata renames, persistent inode numbering (`xino`), and overlay writable quota limits; file sizes are chosen for transparent arithmetic.",
  "ch4.title": "Linux Namespaces: Eight Pairs of Blinkers",
  "toc.ch4": "Linux Namespaces",
  "ch4.law": "A Linux namespace does not clone the kernel. It puts blinkers on a process tree so the exact same kernel tables show two different PIDs, networks, mount trees, or user IDs across the boundary.",
  "ch4.p1": "Once the filesystem is stacked, the kernel sets up visibility isolation across 8 Linux namespaces (`PID`, `MNT`, `NET`, `UTS`, `IPC`, `CGROUP`, `USER`, `TIME`—each visible as an inode under `/proc/&lt;hostPID&gt;/ns/`). By default `runc` unshares the first six so a process thinks it is `PID 1` with its own mounts and loopback interface. Yet <strong>without a `USER` namespace, `UID 0` (root) inside the container is the exact same `UID 0` on the host kernel</strong>.",
  "dg.ns.cap": "Figure 4. Eight Linux namespaces and two-sided mapping: host PID 4821 appears as PID 1 inside, and under Rootless UserNS, container UID 0 maps to unprivileged host UID 100000 via uid_map.",
  "dg.ns.aria": "Diagram: eight namespace badges above two cards comparing container view PID 1 UID 0 with host view PID 4821 UID 100000",
  "dg.ns.in": "Container view",
  "dg.ns.inp": "PID 1 · UID 0",
  "dg.ns.out": "Host kernel view",
  "dg.ns.outp": "PID 4821 · UID 100000",
  "dg.ns.umap": "/proc/4821/uid_map",
  "dg.ns.umaps": "0 100000 65536",
  "ch4.p2": "Two common traps follow directly. First, <strong>namespaces filter views, not kernel existence</strong>: `ps aux` on the host still lists that container process at its real host PID (`PID 4821`), and inside the container `ps` only hides the host's 142 processes if <strong>both</strong> `PID` namespace isolation <strong>and</strong> a fresh `MNT` remount of `/proc` were applied (`2` visible processes). Second, <strong>Rootless UserNS mapping</strong>: writing `0 100000 65536` into `/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/uid_map` lets code inside think it runs as `UID 0` while every host kernel permission check evaluates it as unprivileged `UID 100000`.",
  "ch4.model.title": "Namespace Blinkers & Rootless UID Mapping Inspector",
  "ch4.model.how": "Toggle individual namespaces and the /proc remount switch to inspect how PIDs, UIDs, and visible process counts diverge between inside the container and on the host kernel.",
  "ch4.ctl.pid": "PID Namespace",
  "ch4.ctl.proc": "Fresh /proc mount",
  "ch4.ctl.mnt": "MNT Namespace",
  "ch4.ctl.net": "NET Namespace",
  "ch4.ctl.user": "USER Namespace (Rootless)",
  "ch4.cv.aria": "Eight namespace toggles and two comparison cards showing inside-container view versus host-kernel truth",
  "ch4.cv.inside": "Inside container view",
  "ch4.cv.outside": "Host kernel reality",
  "ch4.read.pid": "Container PID / Host PID",
  "ch4.read.uid": "Inside UID → Host UID",
  "ch4.read.procs": "Processes visible via ps",
  "ch4.read.ns": "Active namespaces",
  "ch4.say.rootful-ok": "The process sees itself as PID {inPid} (really host PID {hostPid}) and sees only {procs} container processes; yet without USER namespace, inside UID 0 equals <em>host UID {uidOut}</em>—any path breakout grants real host root.",
  "ch4.say.rootless": "With Rootless USER namespace active, the process keeps UID 0 and PID {inPid} inside, while the host kernel enforces permissions as unprivileged <em>UID {uidOut}</em>.",
  "ch4.say.proc-leak": "PID namespace is enabled, yet without remounting <code>/proc</code> inside MNT namespace, running <code>ps</code> still reveals all <em>{procs} host processes</em>!",
  "ch4.say.host-pid": "With PID namespace off (like <code>--pid=host</code>), the container exposes its real <em>host PID {hostPid}</em> and can inspect or signal {procs} host processes directly.",
  "ch4.say.no-mnt": "Without MNT namespace isolation, the container shares the host's entire mount tree directly; real host UID is {uidOut}.",
  "ch4.p3": "Notice the interaction between `PID` and `/proc`: unsharing `PID` alone without remounting `/proc` leaves all 142 host processes readable in `/proc` even though `getpid()` returns `1`, whereas combining `PID`, `MNT`, and a fresh `/proc` mount leaves only 2 container processes visible.",
  "ch4.limit": "The model leaves out: `setns()` joining an existing container (`docker exec`), multi-range `/etc/subuid` allocations, and `TIME` namespace monotonic/boottime offsets.",
  "ch5.title": "Container Networking: `veth`, `bridge`, and NAT",
  "toc.ch5": "Container Networking",
  "ch5.law": "A fresh `NET` namespace starts with only a loopback `lo` interface. Containers reach the outside world across a virtual patch cable (`veth` pair) punched through the namespace wall into a host Linux `bridge` and `nftables` NAT rules.",
  "ch5.p1": "Once isolated into its own `NET` namespace, a process has no route out. The runtime creates a `veth` pair—two linked virtual Ethernet devices in the kernel where every frame pushed into one end immediately arrives at the other—moves one end into the container as `eth0` (`172.17.0.2`), attaches the host end to the software switch `docker0` (`172.17.0.1`), and programs `nftables`/`iptables` `MASQUERADE` rules plus `conntrack` state to rewrite source addresses on exit through the physical NIC (see <a href=\"../../systems/how-a-nic-moves-packets/#ch3\">chapter 3 of How a NIC Moves Packets</a> for driver ring buffers).",
  "dg.net.cap": "Figure 5. Logical packet path across Bridge + NAT inside one Linux host kernel before hitting the physical NIC.",
  "dg.net.aria": "Diagram: container eth0 across veth pair into host bridge docker0, nftables conntrack NAT, and physical NIC",
